Expose the host's disc shelf to the guest as a vendor ATAPI command
(doc 07's shared disc shelf, doc 17's ATAPI drive; protocol in
cdshelf/cdshelf_proto.h).

The in-guest CDSHELF program has to run on DOS, Win98 and XP, which rules
out most transports: XP blocks ring-3 port I/O, DOS has no networking to
speak of, and the d3dpt device (doc 14) is XP-only. What all three do
have is a way to send a raw command to their own optical drive - direct
ATAPI PIO on DOS (as tools/atapi-guest-test.py already does), ASPI on
Win98, SPTI on XP - and we own that drive's firmware. So the shelf is a
vendor-specific opcode (0xD0, in MMC's vendor range) on the drive itself:
no new device, no driver to install in the guest, and a drive without a
shelf answers ILLEGAL REQUEST exactly as it should.

ide-cd gains shelf=<file>, a flat "<label>\t<path>" line file the
launcher writes next to the machine's monitor socket and rewrites
whenever the user changes the shelf, so a disc added while the guest runs
shows up in its next listing. Deliberately not the launcher's own
discs.toml: this side is C, and a tab-separated line file is a parser you
can read in one sitting.

Subcommands: LIST (data-in, a fixed-stride header + entries so the DOS
build can walk the reply with an index register rather than a parser),
LOAD <slot>, EJECT. LOAD and EJECT run the medium change from a bottom
half rather than inline - changing the medium drains and reopens the very
drive whose command is still executing - which is also how a real drive
behaves: the command returns, the tray moves after, and the guest picks
the change up as the UNIT ATTENTION blockdev-change-medium raises on its
next command.

The opcode is CONDDATA, not a plain data command: only LIST transfers
anything, and a guest sending LOAD or EJECT through SPTI or ASPI
(guest-tools/src/cdshelf.c) legitimately leaves the byte count limit at
zero, which the generic check aborts at the ATA level. LIST validates it
itself.

A LOAD of a disc the host cannot open is refused with 02/3A rather than
accepted and then dropped in the bottom half, where the only trace would
be a warning on the host's stderr while the guest believes the swap
happened.

The new IDEState fields are not migrated, like patch 51's.

A shelf entry names a medium, not always a file: `isodir:<dir>` is a host
directory the drive generates an ISO 9660 volume from as the guest reads
it (M5g), and it is what the launcher writes for a shared folder. The
existence checks strip that prefix before asking the host — without it
every shared folder lists as MISSING and every LOAD of one is refused,
which is what the guest shelf test found.

Which disc is in the drive is worked out on every listing from the
medium itself (cdimage_medium_path, compared by inode where the host has
one), never remembered from the last LOAD: the bundle's boot disc is in
the tray before the guest sends anything, the launcher's own Insert
swaps it over QMP, and a shelf rewritten in a new order would leave a
remembered slot pointing at the wrong disc.

diff --git a/hw/ide/atapi.c b/hw/ide/atapi.c
index 2f39dfbdee..c0cef9c65b 100644
--- a/hw/ide/atapi.c
+++ b/hw/ide/atapi.c
@@ -31,6 +31,11 @@
 #include "ide-internal.h"
 #include "trace.h"
 #include "qemu/timer.h"
+/* win98-xp-virt (patch 52): the disc shelf's medium change (cdshelf/cdshelf_proto.h) */
+#include "qapi/error.h"
+#include "qapi/qapi-commands-block.h"
+#include "qemu/error-report.h"
+#include "qemu/main-loop.h"
 #ifdef CONFIG_CDIMAGE
 /* win98-xp-virt (patch 51, doc 17 §5.3): the disc model behind a cdimage medium */
 #include "block/cdimage.h"
@@ -2161,6 +2166,286 @@ enum {
     CONDDATA = 0x08,
 };
 
+/*
+ * win98-xp-virt (patch 52): the disc shelf, as a vendor ATAPI command.
+ *
+ * The guest program that lists and swaps discs (CDSHELF) has to run on
+ * DOS, Win98 and XP. What all three have in common is a way to send a
+ * raw command to their own optical drive — direct ATAPI PIO, ASPI, SPTI
+ * — and we own that drive's firmware. So the shelf is a vendor-specific
+ * opcode here rather than a new device or a driver in the guest. The
+ * constants below are the C half of cdshelf/cdshelf_proto.h; keep them
+ * in step with it (and with the DOS program, which spells them out).
+ */
+#define CDSHELF_OPCODE            0xd0
+#define CDSHELF_PROTO_VERSION     1
+#define CDSHELF_SUB_LIST          0x00
+#define CDSHELF_SUB_LOAD          0x01
+#define CDSHELF_SUB_EJECT         0x02
+#define CDSHELF_LABEL_MAX         64
+#define CDSHELF_LIST_HEADER_SIZE  12
+#define CDSHELF_ENTRY_SIZE        68
+#define CDSHELF_NO_SLOT           0xffff
+#define CDSHELF_FLAG_LOADED       0x01
+#define CDSHELF_FLAG_MISSING      0x02
+#define CDSHELF_MAX_ENTRIES       256
+
+/* One shelf line: "<label>\t<path>\n" (cdshelf/cdshelf_proto.h). */
+typedef struct CdshelfEnt {
+    char *label;
+    char *path;
+} CdshelfEnt;
+
+/*
+ * Read the shelf file. Re-read on every command rather than cached at
+ * realize: the launcher rewrites this file when the user adds a disc,
+ * and a shelf that only showed what existed at boot would be a worse
+ * feature than none. It is a handful of lines; this is not a hot path.
+ */
+static GArray *cdshelf_read(IDEState *s)
+{
+    GArray *ents = g_array_new(false, false, sizeof(CdshelfEnt));
+    char *contents = NULL;
+    gsize len = 0;
+    char **lines;
+    int i;
+
+    if (!g_file_get_contents(s->shelf_path, &contents, &len, NULL)) {
+        return ents;   /* no shelf yet is an empty shelf, not an error */
+    }
+    lines = g_strsplit(contents, "\n", 0);
+    for (i = 0; lines[i] && ents->len < CDSHELF_MAX_ENTRIES; i++) {
+        char *tab = strchr(lines[i], '\t');
+        CdshelfEnt ent;
+
+        if (!tab || tab == lines[i] || !tab[1]) {
+            continue;      /* blank line, or no path: skip, don't fail */
+        }
+        *tab = '\0';
+        ent.label = g_strdup(lines[i]);
+        ent.path = g_strdup(tab + 1);
+        g_array_append_val(ents, ent);
+    }
+    g_strfreev(lines);
+    g_free(contents);
+    return ents;
+}
+
+static void cdshelf_free(GArray *ents)
+{
+    guint i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < ents->len; i++) {
+        CdshelfEnt *ent = &g_array_index(ents, CdshelfEnt, i);
+        g_free(ent->label);
+        g_free(ent->path);
+    }
+    g_array_free(ents, true);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The medium change a CDSHELF_SUB_LOAD asked for, run from a bottom half.
+ * Not inline in the command handler: changing the medium drains and
+ * reopens the very drive whose command is still executing. A real drive
+ * behaves the same way — the command returns, the tray moves after — and
+ * the guest picks the change up as the UNIT ATTENTION that
+ * blockdev-change-medium raises on its next command.
+ */
+/*
+ * A shelf entry names a *medium*, which is not always a file:
+ * `isodir:<dir>` is a host directory the drive generates an ISO 9660
+ * volume from as the guest reads it (M5g, docs/tracks/m5-dirdisc.md),
+ * and it is what the launcher writes for a shared folder
+ * (`disc_library::qemu_medium`). What has to exist on the host is the
+ * directory, so the prefix comes off before anything asks about it —
+ * otherwise every shared folder lists as MISSING and every LOAD of one
+ * is refused, which is exactly what happens if you forget.
+ */
+static const char *cdshelf_host_path(const char *entry)
+{
+    static const char prefix[] = "isodir:";
+
+    return strncmp(entry, prefix, sizeof(prefix) - 1) == 0 ? entry + sizeof(prefix) - 1 : entry;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Which shelf slot holds the disc that is in the drive right now, or
+ * CDSHELF_NO_SLOT: the first entry that names the medium's own host file
+ * or directory (`cdimage_medium_path`). Derived from the medium on every
+ * listing rather than remembered from the last LOAD, because the disc in
+ * the tray gets there in more ways than one: the bundle's boot disc is
+ * in it before the guest sends anything, the launcher's own Insert
+ * swaps it through QMP, and the shelf file is rewritten in a new order
+ * whenever a disc is added, so a remembered slot number goes stale.
+ *
+ * Identity, not spelling: the boot drive and the shelf can name one file
+ * two ways (relative and absolute, through a symlink), so where stat
+ * gives inodes the comparison is by inode. Windows' stat reports no
+ * inode (st_ino is 0), and there the strings are compared, which is what
+ * the launcher writes on both sides anyway. `in_tray` (one flag per
+ * entry, ents->len of them) says which entries are that disc: a shelf may
+ * list one file under two labels, and both are in the drive.
+ */
+static uint16_t cdshelf_loaded_slot(IDEState *s, GArray *ents, bool *in_tray)
+{
+    const char *medium = s->blk ? cdimage_medium_path(blk_bs(s->blk)) : NULL;
+    struct stat medium_st;
+    bool by_inode = false;
+    uint16_t loaded = CDSHELF_NO_SLOT;
+    guint i;
+
+    memset(in_tray, 0, ents->len * sizeof(*in_tray));
+    if (!medium || !medium[0]) {
+        return loaded;
+    }
+    if (stat(medium, &medium_st) == 0 && medium_st.st_ino != 0) {
+        by_inode = true;
+    }
+    for (i = 0; i < ents->len; i++) {
+        const char *path = cdshelf_host_path(g_array_index(ents, CdshelfEnt, i).path);
+        struct stat st;
+        bool same;
+
+        if (by_inode && stat(path, &st) == 0) {
+            same = st.st_dev == medium_st.st_dev && st.st_ino == medium_st.st_ino;
+        } else {
+            same = strcmp(path, medium) == 0;
+        }
+        if (same) {
+            in_tray[i] = true;
+            if (loaded == CDSHELF_NO_SLOT) {
+                loaded = i;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    return loaded;
+}
+
+static void ide_atapi_shelf_bh(void *opaque)
+{
+    IDEState *s = opaque;
+    char *filename = s->shelf_load;
+    Error *err = NULL;
+
+    s->shelf_load = NULL;
+    if (!s->shelf_qdev_id) {
+        g_free(filename);
+        return;
+    }
+    if (filename) {
+        /* No format: a .cue/.ccd probes to the cdimage driver (doc 17)
+         * exactly as it does on the command line. */
+        qmp_blockdev_change_medium(NULL, s->shelf_qdev_id, filename, NULL,
+                                   true, true, false, 0, &err);
+        g_free(filename);
+    } else {
+        qmp_eject(NULL, s->shelf_qdev_id, true, true, &err);
+    }
+    if (err) {
+        warn_report_err(err);
+    }
+}
+
+static void cmd_cdshelf(IDEState *s, uint8_t *buf)
+{
+    GArray *ents;
+    guint i;
+
+    if (!s->shelf_path) {
+        /* No shelf on this drive: answer the way a drive that doesn't
+         * know the opcode does, so a guest program can probe safely. */
+        ide_atapi_cmd_error(s, ILLEGAL_REQUEST, ASC_ILLEGAL_OPCODE);
+        return;
+    }
+    if (!s->shelf_bh) {
+        s->shelf_bh = qemu_bh_new(ide_atapi_shelf_bh, s);
+    }
+    ents = cdshelf_read(s);
+
+    switch (buf[1]) {
+    case CDSHELF_SUB_LIST: {
+        int max_len = lduw_be_p(buf + 7);
+        g_autofree bool *in_tray = g_new(bool, ents->len + 1);
+        uint16_t loaded = cdshelf_loaded_slot(s, ents, in_tray);
+        guint fit, n;
+        int size;
+
+        fit = (s->io_buffer_total_len - CDSHELF_LIST_HEADER_SIZE) / CDSHELF_ENTRY_SIZE;
+        n = MIN(ents->len, fit);
+        if (max_len > CDSHELF_LIST_HEADER_SIZE) {
+            n = MIN(n, (guint)((max_len - CDSHELF_LIST_HEADER_SIZE) / CDSHELF_ENTRY_SIZE));
+        } else {
+            n = 0;   /* header only: how the guest learns `total` first */
+        }
+        size = CDSHELF_LIST_HEADER_SIZE + n * CDSHELF_ENTRY_SIZE;
+        if (max_len == 0) {
+            ide_atapi_cmd_ok(s);   /* asked for nothing; no byte count needed */
+            break;
+        }
+        if (!validate_bcl(s)) {
+            break;   /* this one really does transfer data (see CONDDATA below) */
+        }
+        memset(buf, 0, size);
+        stw_le_p(buf + 0, CDSHELF_PROTO_VERSION);
+        stw_le_p(buf + 2, CDSHELF_ENTRY_SIZE);
+        stw_le_p(buf + 4, n);
+        stw_le_p(buf + 6, ents->len);
+        stw_le_p(buf + 8, loaded);
+        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+            CdshelfEnt *ent = &g_array_index(ents, CdshelfEnt, i);
+            uint8_t *e = buf + CDSHELF_LIST_HEADER_SIZE + i * CDSHELF_ENTRY_SIZE;
+            size_t label_len = MIN(strlen(ent->label), (size_t)CDSHELF_LABEL_MAX);
+
+            stw_le_p(e + 0, i);
+            e[2] = in_tray[i] ? CDSHELF_FLAG_LOADED : 0;
+            if (access(cdshelf_host_path(ent->path), R_OK) != 0) {
+                e[2] |= CDSHELF_FLAG_MISSING;
+            }
+            e[3] = label_len;
+            memcpy(e + 4, ent->label, label_len);
+        }
+        ide_atapi_cmd_reply(s, size, max_len);
+        break;
+    }
+    case CDSHELF_SUB_LOAD: {
+        uint16_t slot = lduw_be_p(buf + 2);
+
+        if (slot >= ents->len) {
+            ide_atapi_cmd_error(s, ILLEGAL_REQUEST, ASC_INV_FIELD_IN_CMD_PACKET);
+            break;
+        }
+        /*
+         * A disc the host cannot open (moved, unplugged drive, a typo in a
+         * hand-edited shelf — the same entries LIST flags MISSING) is
+         * refused here rather than in the bottom half. The medium change
+         * would fail there with nothing but a warning on the host's stderr,
+         * and the guest would be told the load succeeded while the old disc
+         * stayed in the tray. "No medium" is the truth from the drive's
+         * side and is what the guest program prints.
+         */
+        if (access(cdshelf_host_path(g_array_index(ents, CdshelfEnt, slot).path), R_OK) != 0) {
+            ide_atapi_cmd_error(s, NOT_READY, ASC_MEDIUM_NOT_PRESENT);
+            break;
+        }
+        g_free(s->shelf_load);
+        s->shelf_load = g_strdup(g_array_index(ents, CdshelfEnt, slot).path);
+        qemu_bh_schedule(s->shelf_bh);
+        ide_atapi_cmd_ok(s);
+        break;
+    }
+    case CDSHELF_SUB_EJECT:
+        g_free(s->shelf_load);
+        s->shelf_load = NULL;
+        qemu_bh_schedule(s->shelf_bh);
+        ide_atapi_cmd_ok(s);
+        break;
+    default:
+        ide_atapi_cmd_error(s, ILLEGAL_REQUEST, ASC_INV_FIELD_IN_CMD_PACKET);
+        break;
+    }
+    cdshelf_free(ents);
+}
+
 static const struct AtapiCmd {
     void (*handler)(IDEState *s, uint8_t *buf);
     int flags;
@@ -2192,6 +2477,15 @@ static const struct AtapiCmd {
     [ 0xbd ] = { cmd_mechanism_status,              0 },
     [ 0xb9 ] = { cmd_read_cd_msf,                   CHECK_READY | CONDDATA },
     [ 0xbe ] = { cmd_read_cd,                       CHECK_READY | CONDDATA },
+    /* win98-xp-virt (patch 52): the disc shelf. No CHECK_READY — listing
+     * the shelf and loading from it are exactly what you want to do with
+     * an empty tray — and ALLOW_UA so a program can run right after a
+     * medium change without a REQUEST SENSE dance first. CONDDATA because
+     * only LIST transfers anything: LOAD and EJECT carry no data, and a
+     * guest that sends them through SPTI or ASPI (guest-tools/src/cdshelf.c)
+     * legitimately leaves the byte count limit at zero, which the generic
+     * check would abort at the ATA level. LIST calls validate_bcl itself. */
+    [ 0xd0 ] = { cmd_cdshelf,                       ALLOW_UA | CONDDATA },
     /* [1] handler detects and reports not ready condition itself */
 };
 
diff --git a/hw/ide/ide-dev.c b/hw/ide/ide-dev.c
index 7e40eb6511..4ee613af07 100644
--- a/hw/ide/ide-dev.c
+++ b/hw/ide/ide-dev.c
@@ -204,6 +204,23 @@ static void ide_cd_realize(IDEDevice *dev, Error **errp)
         }
         ide_atapi_audio_init(s, dev->audio_be);
     }
+    /* win98-xp-virt (patch 52): the disc shelf when shelf= was given.
+     * The ATAPI handlers only ever see an IDEState, so both the shelf
+     * file and this drive's qdev id (which the medium change names) are
+     * copied across here. Without an id there is nothing to address a
+     * change to, so the shelf is listable but read-only — say so once
+     * rather than failing a command later with no explanation. */
+    if (dev->shelf) {
+        IDEBus *bus = DO_UPCAST(IDEBus, qbus, dev->qdev.parent_bus);
+        IDEState *s = bus->ifs + dev->unit;
+
+        s->shelf_path = g_strdup(dev->shelf);
+        s->shelf_qdev_id = g_strdup(dev->qdev.id);
+        if (!s->shelf_qdev_id) {
+            warn_report("ide-cd shelf= without id=: the guest can list the "
+                        "shelf but not load from it");
+        }
+    }
 }
 
